ABOUT = """<h2>About</h2>
<p>Some places on Earth and beyond it belong to no country, or to none clearly: the open ocean, part of Antarctica, a few strips of land that two neighbours each decline to claim, orbit and the Moon. People have tried to set up new countries in these places, with a flag, a constitution, coins and sometimes a platform or raft. This map collects those attempts. It also covers the nearest comparisons on land, such as micronations, special zones granted by a government, and governments that control territory few or no countries recognise. It includes other routes people have used or tried: buying land, secession, coups, moving voters into a town, war and rebellion, seizing ships, escaping slavery to found settlements, and filling the gap left when a state collapses.</p>
<p>Being physically outside a country does not put a person outside its law. A ship is governed by the country whose flag it flies, and a ship with no flag can be boarded by any navy. A spacecraft stays under the country that registered it, along with the people on board. Citizenship, bank accounts, suppliers and radio licences all connect a founder to some government, and many of the attempts on this map were stopped through one of those connections.</p>
<p>A founder could want several different things, and they are not equally hard. People already live at sea. A group can run its own rules among people who agree to them, and it can use its own money among its members. Keeping governments away for ten years is much harder. No new settlement on the open sea, on ice or in space has been recognised as a country by any member of the United Nations. For this reason each kind of place has a separate estimate for each of these goals.</p>
<p>The last part of the list covers combinations and open legal questions, such as launching a rocket from an unflagged platform on the high seas, a ship flagged by an unrecognised state, or a habitat built in space that was never launched from Earth. Many have never been tried. Each writeup says which parts the law clearly settles, which parts are open and who argues what, and which parts are only reasoning from the text.</p>
<h3>How to read the map</h3>
<p>The shaded areas and the list called "Every place, top to bottom" show the estimated chance of success for each kind of place. The percentages change with the menu at top left. Click a place to see the rules that apply there, how a government could stop you, the figures and the attempts made there.</p>
<p>Each mark on the map is one entry. Its shape shows the kind of place and its colour shows how the attempt turned out. Some marks are laws or court cases, included to show where a rule came from. Orbit and deep space cannot be shown on the map surface, so those attempts are placed at the founder's base or launch site on Earth. The Moon and Mars buttons show the landers and rovers that are physically there.</p>
<p>The Patterns box describes what happened across many cases. "Compare every place" opens tables and charts: all the estimates side by side, how far attempts got, who stopped them, what things cost, and a list of what a founder would have to deal with today, from the legal tests for statehood and UN votes to banking, passports, launch licences, radio frequencies and fraud law.</p>
<h3>What the map does not do</h3>
<p>It is not legal advice and it does not recommend any attempt. The percentages are estimates of what could happen. Real attempts have ended in arrests, fraud cases and deaths at sea.</p>"""
METHOD = """<h2>Method and sources</h2>
<h3>What is counted</h3>
<p>The map has __NA__ attempts to set up or hold self-governing territory, and __NL__ entries for laws, treaties and court cases that show where the rules come from. An entry counts as an attempt when someone tried to set up a place, a people or a government outside the usual authority of a country, or set up a private community or special zone that is the nearest comparison. Small claims and hobby projects are included, because they show how governments respond when nothing is at stake.</p>
<p>Entries draw on __NS__ web pages, and each box lists its own. Separate checking rounds compared the entries with their sources and corrected many of them, often over later events. The checks were uneven. Many entries were compared with only one or two of their sources, often by matching dates and figures and reading the doubtful claims, and a small number were not checked at all because their sources could not be opened. The longer explanations of each kind of place draw on these entries and on treaty texts. Some of their general explanation of how the law works is reasoning from those texts, and it is marked as uncertain where no court has ruled. Most of the entries added last rest on Wikipedia, read through a page reader, and most of them were compared with those pages.</p>
<p>Two further checks covered the latest round of about 1,850 entries. A separate reader compared a random sample of 55 entries and 8 essays with their sources and found 63 problems in 36 of the 63 items: unsourced lines, invented or overstated details, a wrong year, wrong pins and overstated law. Those were corrected and the same kinds of error were searched for across the round, but most of these entries have not been read one by one, so similar errors are likely to remain. An automatic comparison then looked for numbers, dates and names in about 1,770 entries that do not appear on their cited Wikipedia pages. It flagged about 200 entries, most of them false alarms, and those flags were reviewed. It cannot catch a wrong claim built from words the page does use, and about 85 entries with no Wikipedia source were outside it.</p>
<h3>The percentages are estimates</h3>
<p>No study measures these chances. The percentages are judgments based on the law and on what happened in past attempts, and other people could reasonably reach different numbers. Each one assumes a serious private group with real money and no government or existing people behind it, starting today. They are rough, so 3% and 5% should be read as about the same, and everything shown as under 0.1% means effectively none, with no real order among those routes.</p>
<p>The main figure answers the question the map is about. <b>Own territory, own laws</b> is the chance that within 25 years the group has all three of these, and keeps them for at least ten years: a defined physical place it controls, with people living there; its own laws and courts as the law that actually applies there, on civil, commercial and criminal matters or nearly all of them; and no outside government's law overriding them in practice, whether by right or because that government has legally handed the power over. Recognition is not required. A route with no physical place scores close to zero however easy it is. So does a ship under a real flag, because the flag state's law applies on board. A zone granted by a host counts only as far as the host really hands over lawmaking, courts and criminal law. It is rated separately from the others, so it is not a sum of them. Where a route works only for an existing people or state, a second figure shows the chance for them.</p>
<p>The other figures answer narrower questions, and a high one does not mean the route gives a territory:</p>
<ul><li><b>Live there undisturbed:</b> getting there multiplied by holding off outside states. A flagged ship scores well here because it accepts its flag state's law.</li><li><b>Physically get there and stay:</b> people living there within 25 years.</li><li><b>Run your own internal rules, use your own currency, hold off outside states:</b> these assume people are already there. Internal rules can sit under another state's law. Holding off means ten years with no government boarding, arresting, closing or absorbing the place.</li><li><b>Recognition:</b> at least one UN member formally recognises the place as a country within 25 years.</li></ul>
<h3>How much of the real world is on the map</h3>
<p>No census of these attempts exists. The totals below are a mix of published counts and estimates, and some cover different periods from the entries counted against them. Among the types closer to an attempt to found a place, the map holds about __COV_HI__% when each type is measured against the lowest published count, and about __COV_LO__% against the highest estimate. The gap comes mostly from estimates, such as up to 1,000 hobby micronations, that no list names. Counted this way, the map can reach 100% of a list and still miss examples the list never named.</p>
<p>Most of that group's entries were added by reading Wikipedia's lists row by row. They were compared with outside datasets by count and not name by name: the 477 separatist movements here are not shown to be the same as the 479 in the SDM dataset. Five types remain mostly left out: special economic zones, Indigenous self-government, flag registers, new states and private cities. With them, the share of everything counted falls to about __COV_ALL__%. War, rebel rule, conquest, protected states, maroon settlements and uncontacted peoples are counted separately because their published totals measure something wider than attempts to found a place. A further __COV_NT__ entries belong to types with no published total, and __COV_OTHER__ more do not fit a type, so neither group counts toward these shares.</p>
<p>About 94% of the entries added in the latest round rest on Wikipedia alone, and many on one row of a list. Wikipedia tagged several separatist lists in September 2026 as possibly machine-written, and its Europe list as mixing autonomy with separatism, so those rows need more caution than the entries with outside sources. Their pins are mostly the approximate centre of a region, and some were placed from general knowledge and not from the page read. Where a source gives no ending, a radio station or project is grouped with those that faded, which is an assumption. Entries with no real location are in the list with no pin.</p>
__COV__
<h3>How far each attempt got</h3>
<p>The steps in each box come from the entry's facts: built, lived in, its own money, its own laws or courts, outlasting a challenge from a government, recognition by a UN member. The last two were decided by reading each case, and they lean in the attempt's favour when the facts are unclear. Outcomes are sorted into five groups. "Stopped or absorbed by a state" includes peaceful divisions by treaty.</p>
<h3>Limits</h3>
<ul><li>Many small claims are documented only by Wikipedia or by their own websites, as each entry's source list shows.</li><li>Positions marked approximate are estimates. Attempts in orbit or beyond are placed at a founder's base or launch site. A few proposals, such as Freedom Ship, never had a site, so their marks are placeholders, and a few entries have no mark at all.</li><li>Some candidates could not be verified and are left out, including Republic of Lomar and Tierra Libre.</li><li>Later events may have changed any entry. The research ended in November 2026.</li></ul>
<h3>Map data</h3>
<p>High seas and the Clarion-Clipperton Zone: Marine Regions, Flanders Marine Institute, CC BY. The band between coasts and high seas is what remains after removing high seas and land, so it combines exclusive economic zones and territorial seas. Land and disputed areas: Natural Earth. Gornja Siga: OpenStreetMap. Antarctic sectors are drawn from the longitudes the claimant countries state, starting at 60°S, the limit of the Antarctic Treaty. Moon: NASA Scientific Visualization Studio CGI Moon Kit. Mars: NASA Mars Trek, Viking MDIM 2.1 colour mosaic.</p>"""
